<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Gadir</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 206 BCE (approx.) · capital Gadir</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The great Phoenician city of Cádiz, gateway to the Atlantic, home of a famous temple of Melqart and one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gadir was founded by settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tyre">Tyre</a> on islands off the Atlantic coast near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalete">Guadalete</a>. Ancient writers dated its foundation to around 1100 BCE, but archaeology places it in the 9th to 8th centuries BCE. Its position beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a> gave access to Tartessian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and Atlantic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Gadir was governed by its own elite under institutions modelled on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tyre">Tyre</a>, and its temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melqart">Melqart</a>, later identified with Hercules, drew pilgrims from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> visited before marching on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 218 BCE. Fish salting, purple dye and long-distance trade made it rich, and it came to lead the Phoenician towns of the south under Carthaginian hegemony.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 206 BCE, after the Carthaginian defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ilipa">Ilipa</a>, Gadir went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under a treaty of alliance. Its citizens received Roman citizenship under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, and Gades became one of the wealthiest cities of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-baetica">Hispania Baetica</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 800 BCE – 206 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:43.78%;width:11.82%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 206 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gadir</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Phoenician religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Phoenician</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gades, Cadiz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-503&amp;focus=gadir" aria-label="Gadir on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4522 860 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gadir lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4842 1116L5259 1106M4842 1116L4881 1072"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5259 1106h0M4881 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4842 1116h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4842 1116h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-503&amp;focus=gadir">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
